---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const circleCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'sqrt(r^2 - x^2)', label: 'upper semicircle', color: '#7c3aed', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '-sqrt(r^2 - x^2)', label: 'lower semicircle', color: '#7c3aed' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'r', label: 'radius r', min: 1, max: 5, step: 0.5, value: 3 },
  ],
  x: { min: -6, max: 6, label: 'x' },
  y: { min: -6, max: 6 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 360,
};

const ccgRelationSort = {
  "prompt": "File each claim under the concept it describes:",
  "buckets": [
    "a function",
    "a relation that refuses"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "returns one y per x",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "a circle",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "a sideways parabola",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "a discipline imposed on relations",
      "bucket": 0
    }
  ],
  "why": "Functions are a discipline imposed on relations, and some shapes — circles, sideways parabolas — refuse it; each x spawns two y-values."
};

const ccgDistEst = {
  "prompt": "Triangle A(0,0), B(4,0), C(2,3). Roughly how long is AC (= √13)?",
  "unit": "units",
  "answer": 3.6,
  "factor": 1.25,
  "why": "√13 ≈ 3.6, and BC is also √13 — two equal sides, so the triangle is isosceles, decided without drawing a thing.",
  "context": "the distance formula is Pythagoras with coordinates"
};

const ccgParenSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these claims about the distance formula is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "The square applies to each whole difference.",
    "The formula keeps its parentheses for a reason.",
    "d = √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²).",
    "(x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)² expands to x₂² − x₁² + y₂² − y₁²."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "Squaring each whole difference keeps the cross terms — dropping the parentheses is the most common algebra slip inside an otherwise correct method.",
  "why": "The square applies to each whole difference, which is why the formula keeps its parentheses."
};

const ccgRoadPlace = {
  "prompt": "A 10% gradient climbs 1 m per 10 m. Roughly how many degrees is that?",
  "labels": [
    "0°",
    "10°",
    "20°",
    "30°",
    "45°"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 45
  },
  "answer": 5.7,
  "tolerance": 1.5,
  "why": "arctan(0.1) ≈ 5.7° — the gradient's interpretation is a rate: metres climbed per metre walked."
};

const ccgCaseMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each special case with its gradient:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "horizontal (flat)",
      "right": "gradient 0"
    },
    {
      "left": "vertical (sheer)",
      "right": "gradient undefined — the run is zero"
    },
    {
      "left": "a negative gradient",
      "right": "descends as x increases"
    }
  ],
  "why": "They are not the same and not interchangeable — a vertical line is also the unique line that is not a function, the circle-plot's problem in miniature."
};

const ccgTariffEst = {
  "prompt": "A mobile tariff costs 5 + 0.10x. At what usage does the cost reach £15?",
  "unit": "units",
  "answer": 100,
  "factor": 1.2,
  "why": "15 = 5 + 0.10x gives x = 100 — the intercept 5 is the standing charge, the gradient 0.10 the per-unit rate.",
  "context": "two tariffs plotted together cross exactly where one becomes cheaper"
};

const ccgPerpPredict = {
  "q": "The gradient perpendicular to m = 2 is…",
  "choices": [
    "−½ — the negative reciprocal",
    "−2 — just flip the sign",
    "½ — just flip the fraction"
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "why": "Flip the fraction as well as the sign: a quarter-turn swaps horizontal and vertical and flips direction, giving −a/b from b/a. The fast self-check is the product, which must equal −1 exactly."
};

const ccgCircleCloze = {
  "text": "A circle is the set of points a fixed distance r from a centre (a, b) — and the distance formula, squared, is its equation: (x − %%0%%)² + (y − %%1%%)² = %%2%%.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "a"
    ],
    [
      "b"
    ],
    [
      "r²",
      "r^2",
      "r2"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "The centre's two coordinates, and the radius's fate after squaring."
};

const ccgSquareOrder = {
  "prompt": "Rebuild how x² + y² − 6x + 4y − 12 = 0 gives up its centre and radius — what comes first:",
  "items": [
    "Group: (x² − 6x) + (y² + 4y) = 12",
    "Complete: (x − 3)² − 9 + (y + 2)² − 4 = 12",
    "Result: (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25",
    "Read off: centre (3, −2), radius 5"
  ],
  "why": "The general form camouflages the centre — completing the square uncovers it, and every circle question in disguise becomes algebra."
};

const ccgSecantBranch = {
  "root": "Substituting y = x + 1 into x² + y² = 25 gives x² + x − 12 = 0, factoring as (x + 4)(x − 3). What is the geometry?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Classify the meeting:",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Two crossings — a secant",
          "next": "secant",
          "feedback": "Right: the line crosses at (3, 4) and (−4, −3), both checkable on the circle (9 + 16 = 25 ✓)."
        },
        {
          "label": "One crossing — a tangent",
          "next": "tangent",
          "feedback": "A tangent needs a repeated root — discriminant zero, the algebraic fingerprint of tangency."
        },
        {
          "label": "None — a clean miss",
          "next": "miss",
          "feedback": "Zero solutions means the line never reaches the circle."
        }
      ]
    },
    "secant": {
      "text": "Two solutions, secant; one, tangent; zero, a clean miss."
    },
    "tangent": {
      "text": "The discriminant's value literally classifies the geometry."
    },
    "miss": {
      "text": "Algebra reading geometry, in both directions."
    }
  },
  "why": "Intersecting a line with a circle means substituting and solving a quadratic — and the root count classifies the meeting."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/intermediate/circles-and-coordinate-geometry">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate lesson in Mathematics, and it stands on three foundational
    lessons: "Angles, Triangles &amp; Euclidean Geometry" (above all the Pythagorean
    theorem), "Linear Functions" (how straight lines graph), and "Quadratics and
    Parabolas" (completing the square returns near the end). Bring those and everything
    here is buildable on the spot.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>compute distances and midpoints from coordinates, and say why the distance formula is Pythagoras in disguise;</li>
    <li>calculate a gradient, read it as a rate, and handle the horizontal and vertical special cases;</li>
    <li>write a line's equation from two points or from a point and a gradient, and test lines for parallel and perpendicular;</li>
    <li>read a circle's equation, complete the square to uncover a hidden centre and radius, and intersect lines with circles algebraically.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Descartes' move (1637) — developed in parallel by Pierre de Fermat — was to glue algebra
    onto geometry: every point is a pair of numbers, every line and curve is an
    <strong>equation</strong>. Suddenly geometric questions become algebraic manipulations and
    vice versa, and neither subject ever recovered its independence. This chapter builds the
    glue itself: how to measure distances and midpoints, how slopes work, what makes two lines
    parallel or perpendicular, and how the humble circle earns its equation. Everything here
    reappears in the derivative (tangents are slopes), in physics (orbits are ellipses), and in
    every graph you will ever read.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="x² + y² = r² drawn as two square-root halves — drag the radius" widget="plotter" config={circleCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag the radius slider and watch both semicircle
    halves stretch together: one number, the radius, controls the whole shape. Read the
    equation as a distance condition: every plotted point sits exactly r units from the
    origin, which is why a single function value cannot produce both the upper and the
    lower arc.
  </p>

  <p>
    Note the graph needs two curve pieces, because a function may return only one y per x — a
    circle is a <em>relation</em>, not a function. That small annoyance teaches something real:
    functions are a discipline imposed on relations, and some shapes (circles, sideways
    parabolas) refuse it. Keep the distinction in your pocket; it becomes central when
    transformations of graphs arrive.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="function or relation that refuses — file each shape" widget="sort-buckets" config={ccgRelationSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> coordinates turn points into number pairs and curves into
    equations, and the circle is a relation that refuses the one-y-per-x rule. The first
    payoffs of the glue are measurements: distance and midpoint.
  </p>

  <h2>Distance and midpoint: Pythagoras, relocated</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>distance formula</strong> is the Pythagorean theorem with coordinates: between
    (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) the horizontal leg is (x₂ − x₁), the vertical leg is (y₂ − y₁), so
    d = √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²). Worked example: is the triangle A(0,0), B(4,0), C(2,3)
    isosceles? AB = 4; AC = √(4 + 9) = √13; BC = √(4 + 9) = √13 — two equal sides, so yes, and
    we never drew a thing. Coordinates turn "is this shape special?" into arithmetic, which is
    why geometry proofs migrated to coordinates after 1637. The <strong>midpoint</strong> is
    the average of the coordinates: ((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2) — the balance point of the segment.
    Real use: a phone network places a mast equidistant from two villages by finding their
    midpoint, then the <strong>perpendicular bisector</strong> (the locus of all points equally
    distant from both) — the same locus idea, iterated over many villages, builds a Voronoi
    diagram, the shape of every coverage map, and of the boundaries between cell masts.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="call the side length" widget="estimate" config={ccgDistEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "squaring the legs means squaring each coordinate on
  its own, so (x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)² becomes x₂² − x₁² + y₂² − y₁²." The square applies
  to each whole difference, which is why the formula keeps its parentheses; dropping them
  is the most common algebra slip inside an otherwise correct method.</p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the algebra slip" widget="spot-error" config={ccgParenSpot} />

  <h2>Gradient: slope as a number</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>gradient</strong> of the line through two points is m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁) —
    rise over run, the same number however far apart the points are (that invariance is what
    makes the line <em>straight</em>). Its interpretation is a rate: metres climbed per metre
    walked, cost per unit, speed on a distance-time graph. Road signs use it directly: a 10%
    gradient climbs 1 m per 10 m — about 5.7°. Worked comparison: which is steeper, y = 2x + 1
    or the line through (0, 4) and (3, 10)? The second has gradient 6/3 = 2 — a tie; but
    through (0, 4) and (3, 13) it would be 3, steeper. Negative gradients descend; a horizontal
    line has gradient 0; a <strong>vertical line has undefined gradient</strong> (the run is
    zero, and dividing by zero is the crime) — which is why its equation is x = k, having no
    slope to speak of.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="drop the marker — the road sign's angle" widget="place" config={ccgRoadPlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> mixing up the two special cases. Horizontal (flat) means
  gradient <strong>0</strong>; vertical (sheer) means gradient <strong>undefined</strong>. They
  are not the same and not interchangeable — a vertical line is also the unique line that is
  not a function, the circle-plot's problem in miniature.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each special line with its gradient" widget="match" config={ccgCaseMatch} />

  <h2>Lines: from two points to one equation</h2>
  <p>
    Every non-vertical line is y = mx + c, with m the gradient and c the y-intercept — the
    value where it crosses the y-axis. Two facts pin a line down: two points, or one point and
    a gradient. Worked example: the line through (1, 3) and (4, 12) has m = 9/3 = 3; feed one
    point into y = mx + c: 3 = 3(1) + c, so c = 0, and the line is y = 3x. The general-purpose
    form is the point-gradient equation y − y₁ = m(x − x₁), which skips the intercept step.
    Graphs earn their keep in practical situations: a mobile tariff cost = 5 + 0.10x plots as a
    line crossing the cost axis at the standing charge (c = 5) with gradient the per-minute
    rate; two tariffs plotted together cross exactly at the usage where one becomes cheaper —
    "break-even" is an intersection, solved by setting two equations equal. This is E-strand
    "graphs in practical situations" in its purest form: read the gradient, read the intercept,
    and the story is told.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the usage that reaches £15" widget="estimate" config={ccgTariffEst} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two points fix a line, and one line's equation already tells a
    tariff story, a road sign or a physics graph depending on the labels. Now two lines get
    related to each other, which is where the gradient earns its geometric meaning.
  </p>

  <h2>Parallel and perpendicular: gradients in relation</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Parallel lines have equal gradients</strong> — same steepness, different intercept,
    never meeting: y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x − 5 are parallel (and their constant gap is why
    railway tracks are the textbook icon). <strong>Perpendicular lines have gradients whose
    product is −1</strong>: m₂ = −1/m₁. Why? Sketch it: the segment from (0,0) to (a, b) has
    gradient b/a; rotating it a quarter-turn swaps the roles of horizontal and vertical and
    flips the direction, giving gradient −a/b — the negative reciprocal. (Trigonometry says it
    in one line: a 90° rotation turns angle θ into θ + 90°, and tan(θ + 90°) = −1/tan θ.)
    Worked example: find the line through (2, 3) perpendicular to y = 2x + 1. New gradient:
    −½. Then y − 3 = −½(x − 2), i.e. y = −x/2 + 4. This pair of facts does real geometric
    work: the altitude of a triangle, the tangent to a curve at a point (perpendicular to the
    radius — the fact that makes the widget's circle tractable), the shortest-distance-to-a-line
    construction in navigation and machine learning's least-squares regression all run on
    m₁·m₂ = −1.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "perpendicular gradients merely have opposite signs."
  The rule is the negative reciprocal: flip the fraction as well as the sign, so a
  gradient of 2 needs −½ rather than −2. The fast self-check is the product, which must
  equal −1 exactly.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="bet before you read on — the perpendicular partner" widget="predict" config={ccgPerpPredict} />

  <h2>The circle, algebraically</h2>
  <p>
    A circle is the set of points a fixed distance r from a centre (a, b) — and the distance
    formula, squared, <em>is</em> its equation: <strong>(x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²</strong>. The
    widget draws x² + y² = r² (centre at the origin) and the slider changes r; each x spawns
    two y-values, which is the two-square-root split. Expanding produces the general form
    x² + y² + Dx + Ey + F = 0, in which the centre and radius are camouflaged — and
    <strong>completing the square</strong> (the quadratics lesson's tool) uncovers them.
    Worked example: x² + y² − 6x + 4y − 12 = 0. Group: (x² − 6x) + (y² + 4y) = 12. Complete:
    (x − 3)² − 9 + (y + 2)² − 4 = 12, so (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25. Centre (3, −2), radius 5.
    Every circle question in disguise — "find the centre", "is the point inside?", "where does
    the line cross?" — is now algebra. Intersecting a line with a circle means substituting
    the line's equation into the circle's and solving the resulting quadratic: two crossings
    (secant), one (tangent — discriminant zero, the algebraic fingerprint of tangency), or
    none.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the blanks — the circle's equation" widget="cloze" config={ccgCircleCloze} />
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the completing-the-square uncovering" widget="order" config={ccgSquareOrder} />
  <p>
    One full intersection, with numbers. Where does y = x + 1 meet x² + y² = 25? Substitute:
    x² + (x + 1)² = 25, so 2x² + 2x − 24 = 0, i.e. x² + x − 12 = 0, factoring as (x + 4)(x − 3)
    = 0. The line crosses at (3, 4) and (−4, −3) — both checkable on the circle (9 + 16 = 25 ✓).
    If the quadratic had yielded a single repeated root instead, the line would be a tangent —
    the discriminant's value literally classifying the geometry: two solutions, secant; one,
    tangent; zero, a clean miss. Algebra reading geometry, in both directions.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="the line meets the circle" widget="branch" config={ccgSecantBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "in the expanded form x² + y² + Dx + Ey + F = 0, the
  centre is (D, E)." The general form camouflages the centre: completing the square shows
  it sits at (−D/2, −E/2), with the radius coming from whatever is left over. In the
  worked example above, the coefficient −6 on x puts the centre's x-coordinate at 3, not
  at −6.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the distance and the midpoint between (0, 0) and (3, 4).</li>
    <li>Why is a horizontal line's gradient 0 while a vertical line's gradient is undefined?</li>
    <li>Find the centre and radius of the circle x² + y² − 4x + 2y − 4 = 0.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Distance = √(9 + 16) = 5; midpoint = (1.5, 2). If you
    squared each coordinate separately, you dropped the parentheses. (2) Horizontal: the
    rise is 0, and 0 divided by any run is 0. Vertical: the run is 0, and dividing by zero
    is the crime — no number answers it. (3) Complete the square twice: (x − 2)² − 4 +
    (y + 1)² − 1 = 4, so (x − 2)² + (y + 1)² = 9: centre (2, −1), radius 3.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Coordinates glue algebra onto geometry: points are number pairs, curves are equations.</li>
    <li>Distance is Pythagoras with coordinates; the midpoint is the average of the coordinates.</li>
    <li>Gradient is rise over run and reads as a rate; horizontal lines have gradient 0, vertical lines undefined.</li>
    <li>Parallel lines share a gradient; perpendicular gradients multiply to −1.</li>
    <li>A circle's equation is a distance condition: (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r².</li>
    <li>Completing the square recovers centre and radius from the camouflaged general form.</li>
    <li>Intersecting a line with a circle substitutes and solves a quadratic: two roots a secant, one a tangent, none a miss.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate Mathematics shelf puts the bridge to work at once: "Trigonometry:
    Sines &amp; Cosines" measures the angles this coordinate system makes available, and
    "The Derivative" turns the gradient of a curve into calculus by asking what the slope
    becomes as two points close in on each other.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The other conics: the same trick, three more times.</strong> Each classic curve is
    one distance condition, written algebraically. <strong>Parabola</strong>: distances to a
    point (focus) and a line (directrix) are equal — the shape that focuses parallel rays to
    one point, hence satellite dishes and headlight reflectors. <strong>Ellipse</strong>: the
    sum of distances to two foci is constant — whispering galleries and Kepler's orbits (the
    planets' paths, with the Sun at one focus). <strong>Hyperbola</strong>: the
    <em>difference</em> of distances is constant — the geometry behind LORAN navigation and
    the sound-shift of a passing train. One formula pattern (distance condition, squared,
    simplified) generates them all; the circle is just the case where the two foci coincide.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Loci: geometry as conditions.</strong> The deepest habit analytic geometry teaches
    is thinking of curves as <em>answers to conditions</em>: a perpendicular bisector is the
    condition "equally distant from A and B"; an angle bisector is "equally distant from two
    lines"; a circle is "at distance r from C". Exam questions ("sketch the set of points…")
    test exactly this translation, and so does applied mathematics: GPS positioning is
    intersecting spheres of the form (distance condition), Voronoi diagrams are intersecting
    perpendicular bisectors, and the "Apollonius circle" (points whose distances to two foci
    have a fixed ratio) quietly underlies perspective drawing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A full exam-style walkthrough.</strong> Perpendicular bisectors combine the whole
    toolkit in four moves: given A(1, 2) and B(5, 8), the midpoint is (3, 5); the gradient of
    AB is 6/4 = 3/2; so the bisector's gradient is −2/3; and its equation is
    y − 5 = −⅔(x − 3). Ask "how far is C(7, 5) from it?" and the same machinery extends: the
    shortest distance from a point to a line runs along the perpendicular, and finding where
    that perpendicular through C meets the bisector is two linear equations solved
    simultaneously. Notice what happened: a geometry problem ("distance from a point to a
    line") became a computation — no protractor, no scale drawing, no measurement error. That
    replaceability of drawing by calculation is the entire value of Descartes' bridge, and it
    is why every geometric fact from now on comes with an equation attached.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Two inventors, one priority muddle.</strong> Descartes published <em>La
    Géométrie</em> in 1637 as an appendix to his <em>Discourse on Method</em>; Fermat's
    manuscript on analytic geometry circulated around 1629–36 but was printed late. Descartes
    got the credit and the coordinate system named after him; historians now credit both. The
    deeper point is historical: algebra had to <em>mature</em> (notation for unknowns,
    equations as objects) before coordinates could happen — the same two centuries that gave
    calculus its runway. Analytic geometry is the hinge between them: with curves as equations,
    Newton's question "at what rate does this curve change?" became askable at all, and the
    derivative chapter exists because a line's gradient could first be computed from a
    formula.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where it goes next.</strong> Three dimensions extends everything by one
    coordinate: distance grows a (z₂ − z₁)² term, the sphere replaces the circle with the same
    completing-the-square move, and the plane becomes the flat case of computer graphics'
    projective geometry. Vectors upgrade points-and-directions into the language physics uses
    for forces (the projectile lesson is already vector thinking), and linear algebra turns
    "intersect two lines" into the machinery behind every 3-D game engine. The coordinate
    bridge you built in this chapter is the one the rest of mathematics crosses.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Algebra and Trigonometry (Analytic Geometry, Conic Sections); Descartes, La Géométrie (1637); Boyer, History of Analytic Geometry (Fermat–Descartes priority). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
